0..95 Comment "//! One function of a buddy allocator, popping a free block, meets its free-list specification."
96..99 Comment "//!"
100..323 Comment "//! Corten\'s case study verifies a buddy allocator against a representation predicate over Rust\'s value forest. This is its second rung after Corten\'s `example`, a function that reads, matches on and writes through a place."
324..327 Comment "//!"
328..638 Comment "//! The body is THIR written as data, its precondition computed by a weakest-precondition fold over it. A read or a write is an existential, a value the place holds with its equation, so stuck places stay in path endpoints. The proof cases on the free list and carries each equation by `Path.ap` and transport."
639..642 Comment "//!"
643..722 Comment "//! - cites: [Farka-2026-corten_foundational_verification_rust_programs], 2026,"
723..742 Comment "//!   § 3 and § 8"
743..793 Comment "//! - see: corpus/model/interop/thir/example.gandr"
794..797 Comment "//!"
798..962 Comment "//! [Farka-2026-corten_foundational_verification_rust_programs]: https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.04372 \"Farka et al., Corten: Foundational Verification of Rust Programs\""
